  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 200 PM MDT Thu Jun 11 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nNorthwesterly flow aloft continues across the region in wake of the \ndeparting upper level troughing. Sufficiently cool air aloft will \ncombine with daytime heating to result in a few showers and \nthunderstorms over the plains, most focused over the Hi-Line east of \nI-15. While gusty winds will be a concern with the strongest \nthunderstorms, the overall environment is no conducive to strong \nthunderstorms.\n\nTroughing then begins to work southward out of Canada Friday, which \nwill result in some afternoon showers and thunderstorms along and \nnear a cold front. This activity persists into the overnight while \nslowly progressing southward over the region.\n\nUpper level ridging slowly build sin from the west later this \nweekend. A few chilly mornings look to be in store through the \nweekend before temperatures trend warmer early next week as the \nridge shifts eastward. A wave looks to flatten the ridge toward the \nmiddle of next week, which will result in increased shower and \nthunderstorm activity, along with breezy winds. -AM \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nGuidance is still a bit split on exact timing of the progression of \nthe front from north to south. The more aggressive guidance moves \nthe front over the plains late morning and early afternoon, which \nwould trend toward a lower probability for thunderstorms. Guidance \nthat is slower with the progression moves it through later \nafternoon, which would allow for greater instability to develop \nahead of the front. Stronger thunderstorms would be favored in this \nscenario. \n\nSunday morning still looks to be the coolest ahead of the building \nridge. Clearing skies should allow most areas to drop into the 30s, \nwith cold prone areas getting closer to 30. Given how short the \nnights are at this time of the year, the duration of any frost or \nfreeze looks to be short lived Sunday morning. -AM",
